<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a portable conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A portable conveyor is a mobile, self-contained conveyor unit on wheels or casters that operators reposition between work areas. They serve truck loading/unloading, seasonal operations, and temporary lines. Portable conveyors include belt, roller, <a href="/conveyor-types/flexible-conveyors/">flexible</a>, and <a href="/conveyor-types/telescopic-conveyors/">telescoping</a> types.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How long can a portable conveyor be?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Length depends on type. Fixed-length portable belt conveyors are typically short enough for one or two people to move. Hytrol, for example, offers its Model B portable cleated incline conveyor in 10, 12, 14, and 16 ft lengths. Telescoping and flexible units reach farther and retract or compress for storage. For longer distances, operators run several portable units in series or install a permanent conveyor.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Can portable conveyors handle inclines?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes. Most portable belt conveyors have adjustable legs or a powered raising device for incline work, which is how they load trucks and mezzanines. Smooth belts hold product only at moderate angles that depend on belt surface and product friction. Cleated belts go steeper: Hytrol rates its Model B portable cleated conveyor at a 45 degree maximum operating angle. Follow the manufacturer load chart, because allowable product weight drops as the incline increases.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What power options are available?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Portable conveyors use three main power sources: AC electric (plugged into facility outlets), battery-electric (onboard rechargeable batteries for cordless operation), and pneumatic (compressed air motors for hazardous or wet environments). AC electric is the most common and least expensive. Battery runtime depends on battery capacity, motor size, and load, so confirm rated runtime against your shift length. Battery units work where no outlets exist, such as outdoor yards, remote docks, and agricultural fields.</p>

</div>
</div>
